<div class="lf-ex"><div class="lf-ex-title">🪤 Trap-Busting Example 2</div><div class="lf-ex-q">A multi-digit number is made up of 5 ten-millions, 3 hundred-thousands, and 8 ones. What is this number?</div><div class="lf-ex-compare"><div class="lf-ex-wrong"><div class="lf-ex-label">❌ Common Mistake</div>50300008<div style="font-size:9pt;color:#991B1B;margin-top:4pt;">The student missed the 0s in the millions and ten-thousands places, only writing the ten-millions digit 5, the hundred-thousands digit 3, and the ones digit 8, leaving out the 0s for the millions and ten-thousands places in between.</div></div><div class="lf-ex-right"><div class="lf-ex-label">✅ Correct Solution</div>
<!-- [v7.0 EN Sync] -->
<div class="lf-ex-title">🪤 Trap-Busting Example 2</div>
<!-- [v7.0 EN Sync] -->
<div class="lf-ex-q">A multi-digit number is made up of 5 ten-millions, 3 hundred-thousands, and 8 ones. What is this number?</div>
<!-- [v7.0 EN Sync] -->
<div class="lf-ex-compare"><div class="lf-ex-wrong"><div class="lf-ex-label">❌ Common Mistake</div>50300008<div style="font-size:9pt;color:#991B1B;margin-top:4pt;">The student missed writing the 0 in the millions place and the ten-thousands place. They only wrote the 5 in the ten-millions place, the 3 in the hundred-thousands place, and the 8 in the ones place, leaving out the 0 in the millions place and the ten-thousands place in the middle.</div></div><div class="lf-ex-right"><div class="lf-ex-label">✅ Correct Solution</div>
<!-- [v7.0 EN Sync] -->
<div class="lf-ex-wrong"><div class="lf-ex-label">❌ Common Mistake</div>
<!-- [v7.0 EN Sync] -->
<div class="lf-ex-label">❌ Common Mistake</div>
<!-- [v7.0 EN Sync] -->
<div class="lf-ex-right"><div class="lf-ex-label">✅ Correct Solution</div>
<!-- [v7.0 EN Sync] -->
<div class="lf-ex-label">✅ Correct Solution</div>
50300008<div style="font-size:9pt;color:#14532D;margin-top:4pt;">1. Write the place value order: ten millions, millions, hundred thousands, ten thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens, ones.
2. 5 ten millions → the ten millions place is 5.
3. 3 hundred thousands → the hundred thousands place is 3.
4. 8 ones → the ones place is 8.
5. Fill the remaining places with 0: the millions place is 0, the ten thousands place is 0, the thousands place is 0, the hundreds place is 0, the tens place is 0.
6. Combine them to get 50300008.</div></div>

50300008<div style="font-size:9pt;color:#991B1B;margin-top:4pt;">The student missed writing the 0 in the millions place and the ten-thousands place. They only wrote the 5 in the ten-millions place, the 3 in the hundred-thousands place, and the 8 in the ones place, leaving out the 0 in the millions place and the ten-thousands place in the middle.</div></div>
50300008<div style="font-size:9pt;color:#14532D;margin-top:4pt;">1. Write the place value order: ten-millions, millions, hundred-thousands, ten-thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens, ones.<br>2. 5 ten-millions → ten-millions place: 5.<br>3. 3 hundred-thousands → hundred-thousands place: 3.<br>4. 8 ones → ones place: 8.<br>5. Fill the remaining places with 0: millions place: 0, ten-thousands place: 0, thousands place: 0, hundreds place: 0, tens place: 0.<br>6. Put them together: 50300008.</div></div></div>


50300008<div style="font-size:9pt;color:#14532D;margin-top:4pt;">1. Write out the place value order: ten-millions, millions, hundred-thousands, ten-thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens, ones.<br>2. 5 ten-millions → ten-millions digit 5.<br>3. 3 hundred-thousands → hundred-thousands digit 3.<br>4. 8 ones → ones digit 8.<br>5. Fill the remaining places with 0: millions digit 0, ten-thousands digit 0, thousands digit 0, hundreds digit 0, tens digit 0.<br>6. Combined, it is 50300008.</div></div></div><div style="font-size:9pt;color:#6B7280;margin-top:4pt;font-style:italic;">💡 Remember to fill in every place value; use 0 for any missing places.</div></div>

7300000<div style="font-size:9pt;color:#14532D;margin-top:4pt;">1. Seven hundred thirty ten-thousands = 730 groups of ten thousand.
2. 1 ten-thousand = 10000, so 730 × 10000 = 7300000.
3. Therefore, the answer is 7300000.</div></div>

7030000<div style="font-size:9pt;color:#991B1B;margin-top:4pt;">Students mistakenly think "seven hundred and thirty thousand" is "seven hundred" plus "thirty thousand", so they write 700 + 30000, which is 7030000.</div></div>
7300000<div style="font-size:9pt;color:#14532D;margin-top:4pt;">1. Seven hundred and thirty thousand = 730 groups of ten thousand.
2. 1 ten thousand = 10000, so 730 × 10000 = 7300000.
3. Therefore, the answer is 7300000.</div></div></div>


7300000<div style="font-size:9pt;color:#14532D;margin-top:4pt;">1. 7.3 million = 730 ten-thousands.<br>2. 1 ten-thousand = 10000, so 730 × 10000 = 7300000.<br>3. Therefore the answer is 7300000.</div></div></div><div style="font-size:9pt;color:#6B7280;margin-top:4pt;font-style:italic;">💡 Be careful: "7.3 million" means 730 ten-thousands, not 7 plus 300,000.</div></div>


